# Gantry — Camera, pointer, and key actions

This file defines how the player operates the game: the registered key
actions, the orbit camera, pointer picking on the build screen, and what each
screen does with them. What the edits themselves mean is `specs/structure.md`
and `specs/program.md`; what each screen shows is `specs/ui.md`.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The runtime layer you write delivers the input: it maps the key bindings below
to the actions the game reads, and it reports the pointer's position in logical
stage units along with its press and release edges.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-3d")}}
The {{engine.name}} engine delivers the input, and its own documentation
defines the API. The game registers each action below against its bindings as
it initializes, through `InitApi.input`, and reads them every frame from
`UpdateApi.input`: `value` for what an action is doing and `pressed` for its
press edge, which the first read of the frame consumes. The pointer is read
from the same place, `pointer` for its position and whether it is held and
`pointerPressed` and `pointerReleased` for its edges, and its position arrives
in logical stage units, on the same axes the readouts are laid out on. A
position on the stage names a line through the yard rather than a point in it,
and the engine's view turns one into the other.
{{else}}
The {{engine.name}} engine delivers the input, and its own documentation
defines the API. The game registers each action below against its bindings from
the game instance's `initialize`, and a player controller is where they are
read: `value` for what an action is doing and `pressed` for its press edge,
which that controller's first read of the frame consumes. The controller reads
the pointer from the same reader, `pointer` for its position and whether it is
held and `pointerPressed` and `pointerReleased` for its edges, and its position
arrives in logical stage units, on the same axes the screen components are laid
out on. A position on the stage names a line through the yard rather than a
point in it, and the world's camera turns one into the other.
{{/if}}

## The actions

| Action | Binding (`KeyboardEvent.code`) | Does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `up`, `down`, `left`, `right` | `ArrowUp`, `ArrowDown`, `ArrowLeft`, `ArrowRight` | menu navigation; camera orbit on the 3D screens |
| `zoom-in`, `zoom-out` | `Equal`, `Minus` | camera distance |
| `confirm` | `Enter` | take the highlighted menu item |
| `back` | `Escape` | leave the screen, as `specs/ui.md` states per screen; abort on the run screen; cancel a pending first node on the build screen |
| `tool-strut` | `Digit1` | select the strut tool |
| `tool-cable` | `Digit2` | select the cable tool |
| `tool-rail` | `Digit3` | select the rail tool |
| `tool-ring` | `Digit4` | select the ring tool |
| `tool-counterweight` | `Digit5` | select the counterweight tool |
| `tool-delete` | `Digit6` | select the delete tool |
| `undo` | `KeyZ` | undo the most recent structure edit |
| `check` | `KeyC` | the static check, on the build screen (`specs/structure.md`) |
| `program` | `KeyP` | build screen to program screen |
| `build` | `KeyB` | program screen to build screen |
| `run` | `KeyG` | start the run, from build or program (`specs/program.md`) |
| `speed` | `KeyS` | cycle `RUN_SPEEDS` on the run screen |
| `mute` | `KeyM` | toggle sound, on every screen |

`ACTIONS` holds those action names in the order above, and `BINDINGS` the keys
that fire each. The tool, undo, check, and screen-switch actions apply where
the table says and do nothing elsewhere; the menus are driven by the four
directions, `confirm`, and `back` alone.

## The camera

The build, program, and run screens show the 3D yard through an orbit camera:
it looks at `CAMERA_TARGET` (`(0, 6, 0)`) from a yaw, a pitch, and a distance.
The camera stands at `CAMERA_TARGET` plus
`dist * (cos(pitch) * cos(yaw), sin(pitch), cos(pitch) * sin(yaw))` and looks
back at the target with `+y` up, both angles in degrees. At yaw `0` it stands
toward `+x` of the target, a positive yaw carries it from `+x` toward `+z` as
every yaw in the game does (`specs/world.md`), and a positive pitch raises it
above the target's level.

| Figure | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Start pose | yaw `CAMERA_START_YAW` (`45`), pitch `CAMERA_START_PITCH` (`30`), distance `CAMERA_START_DIST` (`40`) |
| Pitch limits | `CAMERA_PITCH_MIN` (`10`) to `CAMERA_PITCH_MAX` (`80`) degrees |
| Distance limits | `CAMERA_DIST_MIN` (`10`) to `CAMERA_DIST_MAX` (`80`) |
| Key orbit rate | `ORBIT_KEY_RATE` (`90`) deg/s |
| Zoom rate | `ZOOM_RATE` (`20`) units/s |
| Drag orbit | `ORBIT_PER_PX` (`0.25`) degrees per logical pixel |

`right` raises the yaw and `left` lowers it, `up` raises the pitch and `down`
lowers it, each turning at `ORBIT_KEY_RATE` while its action is held; `zoom-in`
lowers the distance and `zoom-out` raises it at `ZOOM_RATE`. All four run
against the frame's delta time. Dragging the pointer turns the camera the same
way: rightward movement moves the yaw as `right` does, movement toward the top
of the stage moves the pitch as `up` does, and each turns by `ORBIT_PER_PX` per
logical pixel of that movement. Pitch is held inside its limits and distance
inside its; yaw runs free and wraps, staying at or above `0` and below `360`.
The camera pose persists across the three screens and resets to the start pose
when a site is opened.

## Clicks and drags

A press is a click or an orbit drag, told apart by how far it travels. A press
whose pointer stays less than `CLICK_SLOP` (`6`) logical pixels from the
position it went down at is a click, applied at the position it was released
from. A press whose pointer reaches `CLICK_SLOP` from that position is an orbit
drag from that moment until it is released, whether or not the pointer comes
back inside.
A drag turns the camera by the movement it makes after it becomes one; the
movement that carried it across the boundary turns nothing.

A drag edits nothing, and a click edits the structure on the build screen
alone: a press that starts on one of the program screen's tape widgets works
that widget rather than the camera, and the run screen is watched rather than
worked.

On the build screen a click picks by screen distance:

- A node pick considers every lattice node in the envelope that stands in
  front of the camera, projected to the stage; the candidate is the nearest at
  most `NODE_PICK_PX` (`20`) logical pixels from the click, ties going to the
  node nearest the camera. The candidate under the pointer is highlighted
  before the click, so the player sees what a click would take.
- A member pick considers every member's projected segment; the candidate is
  the nearest at most `MEMBER_PICK_PX` (`12`) logical pixels from the click.

A click with no candidate in range does nothing.

## The build tools

The selected tool decides what a click does. Every edit passes through the
rules in `specs/structure.md`, and a refused edit changes nothing on screen
beyond the refusal being visible in the moment (`specs/ui.md`).

- Strut, cable, rail: the first click picks a node and holds it pending,
  visibly marked; the second click on another node places the member between
  them and clears the pending node. A second click on the pending node itself,
  or `back`, clears it without placing. A second click that the rules refuse
  leaves the pending node held, so a slip does not cost the selection.
  Switching tools keeps a pending node; it belongs to the placement, not the
  material.
- Ring: a click places the ring by its base corner at the picked node.
- Counterweight: a click on a node without a counterweight places one there; a
  click on a node carrying one removes it.
- Delete: a click removes what it picks, the nearest in screen distance of a
  member within `MEMBER_PICK_PX`, a counterweight within `NODE_PICK_PX` of its
  node, or the ring within `NODE_PICK_PX` of any of its eight flange nodes. A
  tie goes to the member, then the counterweight, then the ring.

`undo` reverses the most recent structure-changing edit, as far back as the
site was opened, exactly as `specs/structure.md` states.

## Editing the tape

The program screen edits the tape with the pointer and the menu actions. The
exact widgets are the build's design; what the player can do is fixed: add a
step, a move or an action; give a move its commands, each an axis, a target,
and a rate the editor accepts under `specs/program.md`; edit, reorder, and
remove existing steps; and read the whole tape in order. Every edit is
reflected in the state's program (`specs/state.md`).

## The run screen

The run screen takes the camera actions, `speed`, `mute`, and `back` alone:
the player turns the camera and watches rather than steering, with `back`
aborting as `specs/program.md` states.
